    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.test\n\n### Description\n\nI usually use `pre_case` when I need to set up environment for a test case. It can be something a test can't run without (or it can even be dangerous to run it in incomplete environment, if it involves working with a filesystem for example). `mini.test` right now executes test case itself regardless if any errors was in `pre_case`. It's probably debatable, but I think this is wrong. For example in plenary `it` won't be executed in case of errors in `before_each`.\r\n\r\nI realize that it probably won't be changed because it's backward incompatible, but maybe an option similar to `stop_on_error` (but only for `pre_case`) can be introduced?",
